ITEMS = ((('what is an eye injury', 'how do i recognise an eye injury', 'what are the signs of eye injuries', 'what does an eye injury look like', 'how can i tell if it is an eye injury for eye injuries', 'what symptoms occur in eye injuries', 'how to spot severe eye injuries', 'what should i look for in an eye injury', 'how do i know if eye injuries are serious', 'what early signs of an eye injury are'), 'An eye injury may cause pain, redness, blurred vision, swelling or bleeding. Chemical splashes and penetrating injuries are emergencies. Call emergency services for severe symptoms and avoid rubbing the eye.'), (('what should i do for an eye injury at home', 'how do i treat an eye injury', 'what is the first aid for eye injuries', 'how to respond to an eye injury', 'what to do immediately in an eye injury', 'how do you handle eye injuries', 'what helps in an eye injury at home about eye injuries', 'how can i assist with an eye injury', 'what is the best response to an eye injury', 'how should i care for an eye injury'), 'For an eye injury, avoid rubbing, protect the eye with a clean dressing and keep the head still. Remove contact lenses only if they are easy to take out. Call emergency services for severe pain or vision changes.'), (('should i cover an injured eye', 'how to protect the eye after an injury', 'what dressing helps an eye injury', 'should i cover both eyes', 'how to make a protective shield with an eye injury', 'what if the eyelid is cut with an eye injury', 'can i use a plaster near the eye', 'how to keep the head still with an eye injury', 'when to call for help with an eye injury', 'who can assess an eye injury'), 'Protect an injured eye with a clean pad held lightly in place, without pressing on the eyeball. Sometimes covering both eyes reduces movement. Call emergency services if the eye is bleeding or the eyelid is cut.'), (('what if something is stuck in the eye', 'should i remove an object from the eye', 'how to handle a protruding object with an eye injury', 'what if a shard is visible with an eye injury', 'should i press around the object with an eye injury', 'how to shield the eye', 'what if i cannot see clearly with an eye injury', 'when to call emergency services with an eye injury', 'what to tell the operator with an eye injury', 'who can remove an eye object'), 'Do not remove an object embedded in the eye or press on it. Protect the eye with a dressing or cup shape around the object and keep the head still. Call emergency services for urgent assessment.'), (('what to do for a chemical splash in the eye', 'how long to rinse a chemical eye injury', 'should i use water for eye chemicals', 'what if the eye is burning', 'how to irrigate the eye safely', 'should i remove contact lenses with an eye injury', 'what if only one eye is affected', 'when to call an ambulance with an eye injury', 'what to tell the crew with an eye injury', 'who can treat chemical eye injuries'), 'For a chemical splash, rinse the eye with clean water immediately and continue while travelling to hospital. Hold the eyelids open and let water flow from the nose outwards. Call emergency services and tell them what chemical was involved.'), (('can i use eye drops for an injury', 'should i put ointment in an injured eye', 'what if the eye is dry', 'can i use saline with an eye injury', 'what if i have eye medicine', 'should i avoid rubbing with an eye injury', 'what if the eyelid is swollen with an eye injury', 'when to ask a pharmacist with an eye injury', 'how to keep the eye clean', 'who can advise about eye drops'), 'Avoid putting drops or ointment into an injured eye unless a doctor or pharmacist has told you to. Saline may be used to rinse grit, but do not rub. Call emergency services if pain or blurred vision persists.'), (('what to do for a black eye', 'how to treat bruising around the eye', 'can i use a cold pack with an eye injury', 'how long does a black eye last', 'should i apply pressure with an eye injury', 'what if vision is blurred with an eye injury', 'how to protect the area with an eye injury', 'when to seek medical advice with an eye injury', 'what if swelling worsens with an eye injury', 'who can check a black eye'), 'For a black eye, a wrapped cold pack can ease swelling, but do not press on the eyeball. Check that vision is normal. Ask a doctor if sight is affected, pain is severe or the bruising spreads.'), (('how do i treat an eye injury in a child', 'what if a child gets something in the eye', 'how to calm a child with eye pain', "should i cover a child's eye", 'when to call help at home about eye injuries', 'what if the child cannot see with an eye injury', 'how to check for injury', 'what to tell the operator at home about eye injuries', 'how to keep the child still with an eye injury', "who can examine a child's eye"), 'For a child with an eye injury, keep them still and do not let them rub. Call emergency services for severe pain, bleeding or vision loss. A doctor or eye specialist should check any significant injury.'), (('how can i prevent eye injuries', 'what protective glasses help with an eye injury', 'should i wear goggles for diy with an eye injury', 'how to handle chemicals safely with an eye injury', 'what if i play sports with an eye injury', 'how to store sharp tools with an eye injury', 'when to ask about safety equipment with an eye injury', 'what to do after a near miss with an eye injury', 'how to check eye protection', 'who can advise on eye safety'), 'Wear appropriate goggles for DIY, sports and chemical work, and store sharp tools safely. Check protective equipment before use. Ask an employer or safety adviser about eye protection at work.'), (('when should i call emergency services for an eye injury for eye injuries', 'is every eye injury urgent', 'what if vision is blurred at home about eye injuries', 'should i call for a chemical splash with an eye injury', 'what to report to the operator with an eye injury', 'how urgent is bleeding with an eye injury', 'what if the eyeball is cut with an eye injury', 'when to call for a child with an eye injury', 'how to protect the eye while waiting', 'who takes over care at home about eye injuries'), 'Call emergency services for bleeding, severe pain, a penetrating object, chemical splash or sudden vision loss. Tell the operator what happened. Keep the eye protected and the person still until help arrives.'))
